ajñānaṁ tamasaḥ phalam\"}]},{\"section\":\"synonyms\",\"section_title\":\"Books.Verse.synonyms\",\"children\":[{\"type\":\"books.Synonyms\",\"title\":\"synonyms\",\"id\":3920,\"bb\":3448,\"url_path\":\"/en/library/bg/14/16/b3448/\",\"shadow_value\":\"karmaṇaḥ – of work; su-kṛtasya – pious; āhuḥ – is said; sāttvikam – in the mode of goodness; nirmalam – purified; phalam – the result; rajasaḥ – of the mode of passion; tu – but; phalam – the result; duḥkham – misery; ajñānam – nonsense; tamasaḥ – of the mode of ignorance; phalam – the result.\",\"word_for_word_value\":[{\"word\":\"karmaṇaḥ\",\"translation\":\"of work\"},{\"word\":\"su-kṛtasya\",\"translation\":\"pious\"},{\"word\":\"āhuḥ\",\"translation\":\"is said\"},{\"word\":\"sāttvikam\",\"translation\":\"in the mode of goodness\"},{\"word\":\"nirmalam\",\"translation\":\"purified\"},{\"word\":\"phalam\",\"translation\":\"the result\"},{\"word\":\"rajasaḥ\",\"translation\":\"of the mode of passion\"},{\"word\":\"tu\",\"translation\":\"but\"},{\"word\":\"phalam\",\"translation\":\"the result\"},{\"word\":\"duḥkham\",\"translation\":\"misery\"},{\"word\":\"ajñānam\",\"translation\":\"nonsense\"},{\"word\":\"tamasaḥ\",\"translation\":\"of the mode of ignorance\"},{\"word\":\"phalam\",\"translation\":\"the result\"}]}]},{\"section\":\"translation\",\"section_title\":\"Books.Verse.translation\",\"children\":[{\"type\":\"content.Record\",\"title\":\"translation\",\"id\":3921,\"bb\":3449,\"url_path\":\"/en/library/bg/14/16/b3449/\",\"body_value\":\"The result of pious action is pure and is said to be in the mode of goodness. But action done in the mode of passion results in misery, and action performed in the mode of ignorance results in foolishness.\"}]},{\"section\":\"purport\",\"section_title\":\"Books.Verse.purport\",\"children\":[{\"type\":\"content.Record\",\"title\":\"paragraph\",\"id\":3922,\"bb\":3450,\"url_path\":\"/en/library/bg/14/16/b3450/\",\"body_value\":\"The result of pious activities in the mode of goodness is pure. Therefore the sages, who are free from all illusion, are situated in happiness. But activities in the mode of passion are simply miserable. Any activity for material happiness is bound to be defeated. If, for example, one wants to have a skyscraper, so much human misery has to be undergone before a big skyscraper can be built. The financier has to take much trouble to earn a mass of wealth, and those who are slaving to construct the building have to render physical toil. The miseries are there. Thus Bhagavad-gītā says that in any activity performed under the spell of the mode of passion, there is definitely great misery. There may be a little so-called mental happiness – “I have this house or this money” – but this is not actual happiness.\"},{\"type\":\"content.Record\",\"title\":\"paragraph\",\"id\":3923,\"bb\":3451,\"url_path\":\"/en/library/bg/14/16/b3451/\",\"body_value\":\"$25\"},{\"type\":\"content.Record\",\"title\":\"verse_text\",\"id\":3924,\"bb\":3452,\"url_path\":\"/en/library/bg/14/16/b3452/\",\"body_value\":\"namo brahmaṇya-devāya
